.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH voltkit-report 1  "Voltkit 0.10.5" "Voltkit Manual"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
voltkit\-report \- Describe this computer: hardware, drivers, health and recent changes
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvoltkit report\fR [\fB\-\-memory\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
Describe this computer: hardware, drivers, health and recent changes
.PP
Prints a report that\*(Aqs safe to share in a forum or bug report (no serial numbers, names or network addresses), with checks and fixes for drivers, drive health, disk space, temperatures and firmware updates. Also saves today\*(Aqs snapshot, so later reports can show what changed.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-memory\fR
Also show memory slots and upgrade options (asks for the administrator password)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NOTES
.PP
The report leaves out serial numbers, user and computer names, and network addresses, so it can be posted in a forum or bug report. Drive health comes from UDisks2 and firmware updates from fwupd. Each run (and the background service, once a day) saves a snapshot of the hardware, drivers, kernel and firmware in ~/.local/share/voltkit/snapshots, which later reports compare to show what changed. \-\-memory reads the memory slots from the SMBIOS tables, which needs the administrator password.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Copy the report to paste in a forum post:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit report | wl\-copy\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Include memory slots and upgrade options:
.RS 4
.nf
\fBvoltkit report \-\-memory\fR
.fi
.RE
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBvoltkit\fR(1)
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
